From e4c5f2801926ea31b9d2a00ed527194bbedf6697 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artyom Shalkhakov <artyom.shalkhakov@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 16:27:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] NSPopUpButton: content, contentObjects and contentValues
 bindings

A pop-up could not be bound to a list of objects to choose one from:
content was not exposed, NSContentObjectsBinding was not declared,
contentValues only replaced the items by titles (dropping any title that
repeats), and selectedObject selected by tag, taking the object's
-intValue. An application had to fill the items and set the model
itself.

The pop-up now does what AppKit does (checked on macOS 15):

- content gives the items' represented objects, titled by their
  descriptions; contentValues gives the titles, which may repeat;
  contentObjects, when bound, gives the represented objects instead, and
  so what selectedObject is matched against and set to.  The items follow
  the arrays as they change.
- NSInsertsNullPlaceholderBindingOption on any of the three adds a first
  item for nil, titled by that binding's NSNullPlaceholderBindingOption or
  "No Value".
- selectedObject selects the item whose represented object is equal to
  the model's, and choosing an item sets the model to that object (nil for
  the placeholder).  A nil the pop-up has no placeholder for, or an object
  not in the content, is shown as an extra last item ("No Value", or the
  object's description), taken away again once a content item is chosen
  or set; a controller marker selects nothing.
- With content bound, selectedIndex is an index into the content, not
  into the items: the placeholder is not counted, and choosing it sets nil.

contentValues and contentObjects are usually bound through a to-many,
as content to drivers and contentValues to drivers.name. Key-value
observing cannot observe through an array (on macOS too, it raises "is
not supported"), and binding raised here; on macOS the pop-up shows each
member's value and follows both the to-many and its members (checked on
macOS 15). A pop-up binding whose key path passes through an array or a
set now observes the key path up to it, and the rest on each of its
members, moving those observations whenever the to-many changes. A
controller's arrangedObjects, which can be observed, is observed as before.

Bound values reach the pop-up as they are: nil and the markers are not
turned into placeholder strings, which is what a selected object needs.
A pop-up with none of the content bindings keeps its items and
selectedIndex as before, and selectedObject is matched against the items'
represented objects.

Tests/gui/NSPopUpButton/contentBindings.m passes 28 of 28 with this and on
AppKit; without it (the binding names kept, so that it builds), 6 checks
fail and the rest are not reached, the pop-up raising on -intValue.
Tests/gui/NSPopUpButton/contentValuesKeyPath.m binds content and
contentValues through a to-many and changes it and its members; it passes
on AppKit and fails all 6 of its checks without
the change.

